    <p style="font-size: 0.85rem; font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: 1px; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--accent); margin-bottom: 1rem;">Rural Property &amp; Due Diligence</p>

    <h1 style="font-family: 'Cormorant Garamond', serif; font-size: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 2.8rem); color: var(--primary); line-height: 1.25; margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
      Why Buyers Should Think Twice Before Purchasing on a Private Road
    </h1>

    <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: var(--text-light); font-style: italic; margin-bottom: 2.5rem; padding-bottom: 2rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--accent-light);">
      By Dean L. Johnson, Attorney &amp; Real Estate Broker | Sonoma Lawyers Realty
    </p>

    <div style="color: var(--text-light); line-height: 1.9; font-size: 1.05rem;">
      <hr style="border: none; border-top: 1px solid var(--accent-light); margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">

      <p style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
        Sonoma County is full of beautiful properties tucked at the end of long private lanes — vineyard estates, hillside retreats, and rural homesteads that seem worlds away from traffic and noise. But that charming private road leading to the front gate is often the single biggest hidden liability in the entire transaction. As both an attorney and a buyer’s broker, I have seen more neighbor disputes, lawsuits, and financing failures arise from private roads than from almost any other feature of rural real estate.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
        For buyers evaluating rural property in Sonoma County, the road itself can matter more than the house. If access, maintenance, or easement rights are unclear, the transaction can become expensive very quickly.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
        Before you fall in love with a property served by a private road, here is what you need to understand.
      </p>

      <hr style="border: none; border-top: 1px solid var(--accent-light); margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">

      <h2 style="font-family: 'Cormorant Garamond', serif; font-size: 1.8rem; color: var(--primary); margin-bottom: 1rem;">You Don’t Own the Road — You Share It</h2>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
        When a home fronts a public street, the county or city owns and maintains the road. When a home sits on a private road, ownership is typically shared among the neighboring property owners — either as co-owners of the road parcel itself or as holders of easement rights over someone else’s land. That distinction changes everything.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
        Shared ownership means shared decision-making. Every question about the road — when to repave, how wide to keep it, whether to install a gate, who may use it — requires cooperation among neighbors who may have very different budgets, priorities, and personalities. You are not just buying a house; you are entering a long-term partnership with everyone else on the road, whether you like them or not.
      </p>

      <hr style="border: none; border-top: 1px solid var(--accent-light); margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">

      <h2 style="font-family: 'Cormorant Garamond', serif; font-size: 1.8rem; color: var(--primary); margin-bottom: 1rem;">Maintenance Costs Fall on the Owners — and Not Everyone Pays</h2>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
        Private roads receive no county maintenance. Grading, gravel, asphalt, drainage culverts, and storm damage repair are all paid for by the owners who use the road. On a long rural lane, a single repaving project can run into six figures, split among a handful of households.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
        The recurring problem is the non-contributing neighbor. Some owners simply refuse to pay their share. When that happens, the conscientious owners face an ugly choice: pay more than their fair share to keep the road passable, or let the road degrade. Potholes deepen, drainage fails, and winter storms turn the lane into a rutted mess. A deteriorating road drags down the value of every property it serves — and it happens slowly enough that no one ever agrees on when to fix it.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
        California Civil Code section 845 does require easement holders to share maintenance costs in proportion to their use, and it allows an owner to sue a non-paying neighbor for contribution. But that remedy often means turning a practical maintenance issue into a lawsuit against the people you wave to every morning.
      </p>

      <hr style="border: none; border-top: 1px solid var(--accent-light); margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">

      <h2 style="font-family: 'Cormorant Garamond', serif; font-size: 1.8rem; color: var(--primary); margin-bottom: 1rem;">Access Disputes Are Where the Real Litigation Lives</h2>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
        Maintenance fights are unpleasant. Access fights are worse.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
        When your legal right to reach your own home depends on an easement across a neighbor’s land, you are vulnerable in ways that owners on public streets never experience. I have seen neighbors install locked gates, dispute the width or location of an easement, block contractors and delivery trucks, challenge whether an easement was ever properly recorded, and claim that a buyer’s intended use “overburdens” the historical easement. Each of these disputes can become quiet title litigation, injunction proceedings, or prescriptive easement claims — cases that routinely cost each side tens of thousands of dollars in attorney’s fees.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
        And here is the part buyers rarely consider: even if you win, you have spent years litigating for something owners on a public road get for free — the simple, unquestioned right to drive home.
      </p>

      <hr style="border: none; border-top: 1px solid var(--accent-light); margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">

      <h2 style="font-family: 'Cormorant Garamond', serif; font-size: 1.8rem; color: var(--primary); margin-bottom: 1rem;">Lenders and Insurers Notice, Even If You Don’t</h2>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
        Many lenders will not fund a purchase on a private road without a recorded road maintenance agreement among the owners. If no agreement exists — and on older rural roads, one often doesn’t — your financing can stall or fail late in escrow. Trying to get four or five unrelated neighbors to sign a recorded agreement on your closing timeline is not a position you want to be in.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
        In Sonoma County, wildfire adds another layer. Insurers and fire agencies scrutinize access: narrow lanes, weak bridges, and inadequate turnarounds can impede fire apparatus, and that can affect both insurability and premiums in high fire-hazard zones. A poorly maintained private road isn’t just an inconvenience — it can be a genuine safety and insurance problem.
      </p>

      <hr style="border: none; border-top: 1px solid var(--accent-light); margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">

      <h2 style="font-family: 'Cormorant Garamond', serif; font-size: 1.8rem; color: var(--primary); margin-bottom: 1rem;">If You Do Buy on a Private Road, Do This First</h2>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
        Sometimes the right property happens to sit on a private road, and the deal can still make sense — but only with eyes open. At minimum, your due diligence should include a careful review of the preliminary title report and every recorded easement serving the property; confirmation that a recorded road maintenance agreement exists; a close reading of its cost-sharing formula and enforcement provisions; a candid conversation about the history of the road — who pays, who doesn’t, and whether there have been disputes; verification that the easement’s recorded width and location match the road as actually built; and an assessment of fire access and what your insurer will require.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
        If any of those items comes back murky, the purchase price should reflect the risk — or you should walk.
      </p>

      <hr style="border: none; border-top: 1px solid var(--accent-light); margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">

      <h2 style="font-family: 'Cormorant Garamond', serif; font-size: 1.8rem; color: var(--primary); margin-bottom: 1rem;">Why Your Agent Probably Won’t Tell You Any of This</h2>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
        As a lawyer, I understand these risks very well — I’ve spent my career reading easements, litigating access disputes, and cleaning up the aftermath of deals that should never have closed. Most real estate agents will downplay these concerns, and the reason is structural: an agent’s commission is earned only when the deal closes. Every red flag raised about a private road is a threat to that commission. The incentive isn’t to dig into the title report and find the problem — it’s to keep the transaction moving, reassure the buyer that “everyone out here is on a private road,” and get to the closing table.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
        Most agents also simply aren’t equipped to evaluate these issues. Reading a chain of recorded easements, spotting a defective legal description, or assessing whether a road maintenance agreement is actually enforceable is legal work, not sales work. An agent who can’t identify the problem certainly isn’t going to warn you about it.
      </p>

      <hr style="border: none; border-top: 1px solid var(--accent-light); margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">

      <h2 style="font-family: 'Cormorant Garamond', serif; font-size: 1.8rem; color: var(--primary); margin-bottom: 1rem;">The Bottom Line</h2>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
        A public road is boring, and boring is exactly what you want between your driveway and the rest of the world. Private roads bundle shared ownership, unfunded maintenance obligations, and litigation risk into an asset most buyers never think to examine until it’s too late. All else being equal, choose the property on the county-maintained road.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
        When the property you love sits on a private one, that’s when having an attorney-broker on your side of the table matters most.
      </p>

